  "title": "The Draft That Ate the Deadline",
  "family": "fiction",
  "category": "creative-work struggles",
  "premise": "An animator wants to finish a short scene, but each new idea physically adds another page to a growing paper creature. They choose one page with the clearest action, park the rest in a labeled NEXT TIME box, and finish the scene while the creature curls up for a nap.",
  "hook": "The draft has grown legs.",
  "visible_action": "Pages sprout limbs and sprawl across a desk; animator extracts one page; remaining sheets fold into a box; final shot plays on a tiny screen.",
  "intended_takeaway_or_theme": "Choosing what to finish can matter more than generating more options.",
  "gate": "Metaphorical creative process; do not promise productivity outcomes from a single technique.",
